# Architecture

{{ project_name }} is a data project that agents build and operate, with humans owning the boundaries.
Every design choice below serves one of three goals:

1. **One way to do each thing.** One writer, one definition per metric, one entrypoint (`just`). Agents
   copy what they see, so there must be only one thing to copy.
2. **Boundaries are code, not prose.** A rule either has a tool that fails, or it is marked
   *judgment* in `CONVENTIONS.md`.
3. **Every number can be traced.** Snapshot ids, run ids and code versions travel with the data.

## The stack

| Concern | Choice | Why this one |
|---|---|---|
| Storage | DuckLake: Postgres catalog + Parquet on RustFS (S3 API) | multi-table ACID commits, time travel, catalog knows every file (no listing) |
| Query | DuckDB (+ ibis for the semantic layer) | in-process, fast on Parquet, one dialect |
| Transforms | Apache Hamilton over polars | functions as DAG nodes: lineage for free, unit-testable |
| Validation | pandera (polars) via LinkML-generated contracts | one declaration → DDL, schema check, value checks |
| Orchestration | DBOS Transact | durable workflows in plain Python, Postgres-backed, approvals via `recv`/`send` |
| Metrics | boring-semantic-layer + `dataplat.gate` | measures defined once; answers carry provenance or refuse |
| Graph | LadybugDB native files, rebuilt from the lake | Cypher for relationship questions; parity-checked, never a source of truth |
| Ontology | LinkML → Pydantic + JSON Schema | typed action inputs and tool schemas for agents |
| Agents | Claude Code (hooks, skills, rules), ZeroClaw SOPs | same procedures, same gates |

## Data flow

```
 external systems ──▶ sources/ (strings) ──▶ transforms/ (Hamilton, pure, validated)
                                                  │
                                                  ▼
                               pipelines/ (DBOS step: ONE Writer commit)
                                                  │  data + run-log row + commit message,
                                                  ▼  one DuckLake transaction
             ┌──────────────────────────── the lake (DuckLake) ────────────────────────────┐
             │ Postgres catalog: tables, snapshots, _meta.dataset_runs                      │
             │ RustFS: Parquet files (found through the catalog, never listed)              │
             └─────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┘
                           │ Reader pinned at snapshot N    │ nightly: build → parity → publish
                           ▼                               ▼
             semantic/ (BSL) ─▶ gate: Answer | Refusal   graph/ (LadybugDB file, read-only)
                           │                               │
                           └──────────▶ agents ◀───────────┘
                                          │ propose
                                          ▼
                    actions/ (preconditions → human approval → effect once → audit)
```

## Guarantees and the mechanisms behind them

| Guarantee | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| A partition exists iff its run-log row says it succeeded | data + `_meta.dataset_runs` row commit in one DuckLake transaction |
| Re-running a pipeline never double-counts | partition overwrite (DELETE + INSERT, one txn) + deterministic DBOS workflow ids |
| Two agents never corrupt one partition | DuckLake conflict detection; the loser retries on the new snapshot |
| A reader sees one consistent state | `Lake.reader()` attaches at `SNAPSHOT_VERSION n` |
| Nothing writes around the writer | ruff bans + `dataplat lint` DPA001–004, verified by `deep_census` on the box |
| Empty partitions are deliberate | the writer refuses zero rows unless given `allow_empty` + a note |
| Repairs are reversible | witness columns + `pre_snapshot` in the run log + `restore_partition` |
| Metrics don't lie by omission | gate preconditions: complete windows, freshness, sample floors |
| Agents can't leak restricted fields | `forbidden_columns` can't be declared; sources select explicit columns |
| Parallel agents don't collide | worktree ⇒ own namespace (metadata schema, data prefix, DBOS app); `main` refused |
| Telemetry can't leak identifiers | every span and log leaves through dataplat's scrubbing exporters (live-fired in dataplat's tests) |
| A failure or a missed run reaches a human | alerts as code (`just alerts`): failures, new error signatures, one heartbeat per schedule |

## Processes

| Process | How many | DBOS role | Lake | Does |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `{{ cli_name }} worker` | one per namespace, long-lived | `worker` (stable executor id, listens to queues) | read-write | schedules, proposed actions; recovers its own interrupted work after restart |
| `{{ cli_name }} <command>` | any, short-lived | `cli` (ephemeral executor id, no queues) | read-write in pipelines, read-only for questions | runs one workflow to completion, or proposes/approves an action |
| agent sessions (Q&A, MCP tools) | any | none, or `cli` to propose | `connect(read_only=True)` | reads at pinned snapshots, asks through the gate, proposes actions |

An agent never executes a side effect itself. It proposes (`dataplat.actions.propose`). The worker runs
the preconditions and waits. A human approves (`{{ cli_name }} approve <id> --by <name>`). The worker
re-checks the preconditions and runs the effect step. A completed step never runs again. If the worker
dies mid-step, the step re-runs on recovery, so adapters pass `DBOS.workflow_id` as an idempotency key.

## Telemetry

Off unless `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` is set (tests force it off). When it is on, `launch()` installs
OpenTelemetry trace and log providers before DBOS starts, and DBOS's own instrumentation does the rest:

- every workflow and step is a span (`operation_name` = function name, `dbos.operation.type`,
  `dbos.operation.workflow_id`); a failure sets the span to ERROR with the exception message;
- DBOS logs a failed background workflow with its stack trace; warnings and errors from every other
  logger go out too;
- everything passes through `dataplat.observe.scrub` first: emails, UUIDs, 7+ digit runs and quoted
  values become shapes (`+9{12}`), and each span carries `dataplat.error`, the failure's signature
  (empty when it succeeded). Messages still carry counts and shapes only; scrubbing is the backstop.

The resource names the process: `service.name` is the DBOS app name (`{{ package_name }}` on main,
`{{ package_name }}-<ns>` in a worktree), plus `dataplat.namespace` and `dataplat.role`. All of a project's
namespaces report into one OpenObserve stream (`{{ package_name }}`); only main's `service.name` pages.

On the box, OpenObserve runs from `deploy/openobserve` (a Dokploy compose service, data on RustFS; its
README has the steps). Locally, `just observe` starts one in Docker.

`just alerts` prints the alerts this project defines: failed workflows (grouped by workflow and error
signature, reported at most hourly), error signatures new in the last 7 days, and one heartbeat per
schedule (no successful run within its cron interval plus grace). The owner applies them with
`just alerts --apply --destination <name>`, an upsert by name that never deletes alerts it didn't make.

## What runs where

| Stage | Budget | Runs |
|---|---|---|
| Edit (Claude hook) | < 2 s | ruff format + fix, ruff check, `dataplat lint` on the edited file |
| Write time (MCP) | on demand | jscpd `check_duplication`, code search |
| pre-commit | < 10 s | ruff, `dataplat lint`, import contracts, contract freshness, gitleaks, no commits to main |
| pre-push | < 1 min | impacted tests, duplication ratchet |
| CI (PR, merge queue, main) | < 5 min | `just ci`: all static gates, full tests + live-fire, duplication ratchet |
| Nightly | unbounded | full suite, lock drift, dependency audit, duplication report |
| On the box (owner) | — | `just verify-prod`: run-log vs table agreement, freshness |

## Decisions

Platform decisions (DuckLake, DBOS, single writer, native graph over Icebug, namespaces, extension
pinning) are recorded as ADRs in the dataplat repo. This project records its own in `docs/adr/`.
